e were calling into question the  whole democratic process and kind of  undermining  things publicly i always judged that as  something that could never happen in the  united states  and so when i saw early on  you know the president saying publicly  that  you know we don't know if we can trust  the democratic process  it all felt like something i  had seen but couldn't have imagined it  would happen in the united states  david actually road trip to dc his road  trip  started in minneapolis that's where he  lives and  that was ground zero for last year's  protests i began  at george floyd square it seemed like  the most  natural starting point because you know  george floyd was killed on 38th in  chicago and  it sort of rippled across the country in  the world so i thought that that would  be  a really um important  sort of launching point  david said something else that was  really interesting there's this  grassroots effort to preserve the place  where george floyd was killed  as a memorial so david told the  protesters  who still occupy that intersection he  was starting this road trip there  and they said it made all the sense in  the world like where else would you  start  i drove across the state of wisconsin  like hitting very rural areas  you know where i saw during the  elections the bitter divisions between  people who i'd you know grown up with  everything from  the wearing of masks politics  to guns to  you know whose lives matter to science  what i saw in rural wisconsin was  sort of the leftover remnants of the  election  trump campaign posters  trump painted on the sides of bales of  hay  in farm fields it definitely is  i've lived in this area for quite some  time i've never seen a city like this  out on the streets over the last few  days it was difficult to find  many trump supporters but i ran into one  this morning would you be willing to  talk to me for a minute  uh interview he was wearing an nra cap  and we had just passed through one of  the 